<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sándor Petőfi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1823">1823</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sándor Petrovics, Alexander Petrovics</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sándor Petőfi was a Hungarian poet and liberal revolutionary, born in Kiskőrös in 1823 and considered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s national poet. His poems relied on folkloric elements and song-like verse, and he wrote the long fairy-tale epic János Vitéz (1845). On 15 March 1848 he and the Youths of March led the rising in Pest, reading his Nemzeti dal (National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>) and the 12 Points to the crowds and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> them after declaring an end to censorship. He then fought in the Transylvanian army under Józef Bem and was last seen alive at the Battle of Segesvár on 31 July 1849, where he most likely died; his body was never found.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sandor-petofi.jpg" alt="Sándor Petőfi" width="330" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Soma Orlai Petrich · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orlai-petofi1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Petőfi was born on New Year's morning of 1823 in Kiskőrös, where the population was predominantly of Slovak origin. His birth certificate gives his name as Alexander Petrovics. His father, István Petrovics, was a village butcher and innkeeper, and his mother, Mária Hrúz, a servant and laundress of Slovak descent. When he was 15 the family fell into hardship after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> floods of 1838 and a relative's bankruptcy, and he had to leave the lyceum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banska-stiavnica">Selmecbánya</a>. He then held small jobs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> in Pest, taught in Ostffyasszonyfa and spent some months as a soldier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sopron">Sopron</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At Pápa he met Mór Jókai, and in 1842 his poem &quot;A borozó&quot; appeared in the magazine Athenaeum under the name Sándor Petrovics; on 3 November that year he first published under the surname Petőfi. In 1844 he walked from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debrecen">Debrecen</a> to Pest to find a publisher and succeeded. His poems drew on folklore and song-like verse, and in 1845 he wrote János Vitéz, a fairy-tale of 370 quatrains in 27 chapters. The year after meeting Júlia Szendrey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> in 1846, he married her despite her father's opposition.</p><p>On the morning of 15 March 1848 he and the other Youths of March marched round Pest reading the Nemzeti dal and the 12 Points to growing crowds, visiting printers and declaring an end to censorship. Crowds forced the mayor to sign the 12 Points, and the representatives of Emperor Ferdinand signed them as well. His popularity waned afterwards; he ran for election in his native area but did not win a seat, and he criticised the noble-led Assembly's methods. He then joined the Hungarian Revolutionary Army under Józef Bem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-i-of-russia">Nicholas I of Russia</a> intervened to support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, the Transylvanian army was defeated. Petőfi was last seen alive at the Battle of Segesvár on 31 July 1849, and is believed to have been killed there by the Imperial Russian Army; a Russian military doctor recorded an account of his death. His body was never found, and rumours of his survival persisted. In the late 1980s Soviet investigators found archives showing that about 1,800 Hungarian prisoners were marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and alternative theories suggest that he was among them and died of tuberculosis in 1856.</p><p>Hungarian schoolchildren learn some of his poems by heart, and there are 11 Petőfi streets and 4 Petőfi squares in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> alone. His poem &quot;Szerelem, szabadság&quot; is in Chinese schoolbooks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a> composed a piano piece in his memory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sándor Petőfi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sándor Petőfi</b>'s poetry was read aloud with the 12 points in Pest.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The revolutions of 1848 brought Romantic poets into politics: Alphonse de Lamartine directed the foreign policy of the new French republic, and in Pest <b>Sándor Petőfi</b> recited his “National Song” on 15 March.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
